I wouldn't worry about that. Just think about the greater purpose of 
locked doors and keys: You are asking the player to return to 
previously explored areas to unlock doors that couldn't be unlocked 
before. It gives the player an excuse to get to know the map a little 
better, giving a greater depth of exploration. Instead of just 
exploring mile after mile of random caverns, the player will have a 
motivation to remember where the PC has been.

Of course, this only works if the map is large enough for the 
player's memory to be important. If the player can see the map 
entirely in one screen, then the fun of it is lost. Having multiple 
levels does not help, because the player still doesn't have to 
remember anything. All the player has to do is keep going up and all 
the doors that have been past will eventually be encountered again, 
which is boring.

My point is, that in none of this does it make any difference if 
there is a door that can never be opened. It might even help by 
giving the player more to remember. It is certainly not a problem 
that the player doesn't get to explore that area; the player can 
always find more to explore just by going down.

Extrapolating further in this direction, it is very important that 
player can tell which doors go with which keys, such as with the 
color-coding system. That way the player can remember exactly where 
the doors are that can be opened with any key that is found and no 
time is wasted trying every door.

But colors seem a bit unlikely. Surely that will conflict with 
whatever the flavor is in your roguelike. I think it would be more 
satisfying to the player if the door was described in a way that 
hinted at what was behind it, to give the player a greater sense of 
anticipation and reward from finding keys and bothering to go back 
and use them. If you are going to use clever dungeon generation, this 
is what you should do first, especially since it allows you to 
guarantee that the door will be worth opening and not just an empty 
room.


